 2. PROJECT TITLE
Aravalli Eye — A Web-Based System for Detecting Illegal / Unplanned Hill Cutting and Land-Use Changes
3. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY
The Aravalli region around Alwar is an important natural landscape, but changes such as hill cutting, construction, mining and loss of vegetation can slowly change the area. One difficulty is that these changes may happen at different locations and are not always easy to notice from the ground. The proposed project, “Aravalli Eye”, is a web-based change monitoring system focused on the Alwar Aravalli region.

The basic idea is to divide the selected area into smaller monitored zones and compare satellite images from different dates. If a noticeable change in vegetation or terrain is found, the system will mark the location for attention. Citizens can also submit observations through the website. These reports can be viewed along with the satellite-based change information on an interactive map.

A simple priority score will be generated by combining factors such as the level of detected change and the number of citizen reports. For example, a zone with a significant land-cover change and several citizen reports can be marked as “High Priority” and sent for verification. The system is not intended to declare that an activity is illegal automatically. Its purpose is to help identify places that may need field verification.

The project combines satellite change detection, citizen reporting and GIS-based visualization in one system. This can make monitoring easier to understand and provide a useful starting point for future verification and environmental management.
4. BACKGROUND AND CONTEXT
The Aravalli Range is one of the major old mountain systems of India and passes through Rajasthan, including the Alwar region. The landscape around Alwar contains hills, vegetation and areas that are also under pressure from development and other human activities. Hill cutting and changes in land use can be difficult to monitor continuously because the affected areas are spread over a large region.

For a student-level computer application project, the problem can be approached through remote sensing and GIS concepts. Satellite images can provide a way to observe the same area at different points in time. Instead of checking every location manually, a computer system can first highlight zones where visible land-cover or vegetation changes appear. Local observations can then provide another source of information.

The proposed system is therefore designed around three connected parts: satellite-based change indication, citizen reporting, and a map-based dashboard. The final decision about whether a change is legal or illegal would still require proper verification by the concerned authority.
5. PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION AND DEFINITION
5.1 Problem Statement
There is a practical difficulty in identifying and organizing information about hill cutting, construction, mining and other land-use changes across the Alwar Aravalli region. Ground-level complaints may cover only the places that people notice, while large areas may be difficult to inspect regularly. A simple system is needed that can bring possible changes, citizen observations and locations together in one place for easier review.
5.2 Need and Significance
The project is significant because environmental changes in hilly areas can affect vegetation, soil, drainage and the appearance of the landscape. From a computing point of view, the project also gives a practical use of satellite data, image comparison, GIS mapping, databases and web development. The proposed system can help users move from scattered observations to a location-based list of areas that need attention.
